    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d121">

                <pb facs="0346" n="318" xml:id="pg318"/>

                <head>121. Telegram From the Embassy in the Soviet Union
                    to the Department of State<anchor n="0"
                        xml:id="d121-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Moscow</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1962-10-31T14:00:00+03:00">October 31, 1962,
                            2 p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>1149. Policy. Gromyko initiative on Cuba
                    yesterday (Embtel 1139)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d121-f2"/> appears corroborate estimate of Soviet intentions salvage
                    as much as possible of their position in Cuba. In this respect seems to us to
                    reflect same kind of internal pushing and hauling which must have taken place
                    between despatch Khrushchev private
                    letter October 26 and published letter October 27.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d121-f3"
                    /></p>
                <p>Western colleagues incline to view “heads must roll” as result set-back, though
                    neither they nor we are able to identify source of any differences in
                    leadership.</p>
                <p>In any event, we believe Khrushchev
                    personally convinced situation was critically dangerous and had to be
                    liquidated. (We are struck by parallel between danger to USSR in which Cuban gamble eventuated and
                        Khrushchev’s accusations against
                    Stalin in “secret speech” for having permitted Soviet Union come up to threshold
                    World War II grossly unprepared, both militarily and psychologically.)</p>
                <p>On balance we consider selection of Kuznetsov for New York and decision to despatch Mikoyan to Cuba encouraging factors. Seems to
                    us of some importance they chose to send Mikoyan via New York for consultation with Kuznetsov rather than direct, as they could
                    have done (Embtel 1145).<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d121-f4"/></p>
                <p>Factors producing dramatic decision withdraw included not only striking display
                    US determination and unity Western alliances when chips are down, but distaste
                    Soviet populace for Cuban adventures. Sense of relief here following publication
                    exchanges of letters unmistakable.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                            >Kohler</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
